WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.603
A JSON Web Token carries claims that
another

2
00:00:03.603 --> 00:00:06.512
system may use to make security decisions.

3
00:00:06.512 --> 00:00:08.936
For an Express API, receiving readable

4
00:00:08.936 --> 00:00:11.845
claims is only the beginning of
processing.

5
00:00:11.845 --> 00:00:14.560
A JWT becomes trustworthy only after the

6
00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:17.663
application verifies its cryptographic
protection and validates every

7
00:00:17.663 --> 00:00:20.572
claim required by the application's token
policy.

8
00:00:20.572 --> 00:00:22.317
Decoding alone establishes nothing.

9
00:00:22.317 --> 00:00:25.032
An ordinary signed JWT commonly uses the

10
00:00:25.032 --> 00:00:28.038
compact JSON Web Signature format, or JWS.

11
00:00:28.038 --> 00:00:30.559
Its protected header, claims payload, and

12
00:00:30.559 --> 00:00:33.080
cryptographic signature are separated by
dots.

13
00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:35.989
The header and payload use base64url
encoding.

14
00:00:35.989 --> 00:00:38.608
This makes them transportable, not
confidential.

15
00:00:38.608 --> 00:00:42.292
Anyone holding the token can normally read
those sections.

16
00:00:42.292 --> 00:00:45.686
Do not put passwords, private keys, API
credentials,

17
00:00:45.686 --> 00:00:48.595
or unnecessary sensitive personal data
into claims.

18
00:00:48.595 --> 00:00:51.698
The deliberately unsafe example decodes a
token and

19
00:00:51.698 --> 00:00:55.383
immediately assigns the result to the
request's user context.

20
00:00:55.383 --> 00:00:58.873
A decoder can parse client-supplied JSON
without proving who

21
00:00:58.873 --> 00:01:02.558
protected it or whether it belongs at this
API.

22
00:01:02.558 --> 00:01:06.631
A familiar subject or role field does not
change that.

23
00:01:06.631 --> 00:01:09.831
Do not use parsed claims to grant access,

24
00:01:09.831 --> 00:01:12.836
even temporarily, while verification is
still pending.

25
00:01:12.836 --> 00:01:14.970
Define a complete processing boundary.

26
00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:17.685
Extract one token from the expected
transport

27
00:01:17.685 --> 00:01:20.594
and enforce size and basic format limits.

28
00:01:20.594 --> 00:01:23.697
Use a mature library to verify
cryptographic protection

29
00:01:23.697 --> 00:01:26.993
with trusted keys under a server-defined
algorithm policy.

30
00:01:26.993 --> 00:01:29.612
Validate issuer, audience, time
restrictions, token

31
00:01:29.612 --> 00:01:31.842
purpose, and required application claims.

32
00:01:31.842 --> 00:01:34.363
Only then construct an authenticated
context.

33
00:01:34.363 --> 00:01:36.690
Authorization is a subsequent decision
about

34
00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:38.823
the requested operation and resource.

35
00:01:38.823 --> 00:01:43.478
Encrypted JWTs use a different mechanism,
JSON Web Encryption, or JWE.

36
00:01:43.478 --> 00:01:46.193
Encryption can provide confidentiality
within a properly

37
00:01:46.193 --> 00:01:49.005
designed protocol, but it does not remove

38
00:01:49.005 --> 00:01:51.914
claim validation or other required
cryptographic checks.

39
00:01:51.914 --> 00:01:54.823
This course focuses on signed access
tokens.

40
00:01:54.823 --> 00:01:57.247
Their integrity supports authentication;
encoding alone

41
00:01:57.247 --> 00:01:59.380
provides neither authentication nor
secrecy.

